==============================================================================
PART 4b -- THE FAMILY, BACKWARD: 1729 LIVES AT n = -1
==============================================================================
The homogeneous recurrence u_{n+3} = 82u_{n+2} + 82u_{n+1} - u_n has unit
leading AND trailing coefficients, so it runs backwards over Z:
u_n = 82u_{n+2} + 82u_{n+1} - u_{n+3}.  (For n < 0 these are NOT power-series
coefficients -- the series ones vanish -- but the canonical bi-infinite
extension of the homogeneous recurrence, as in Ono--Trebat-Leder.)
  n = -3: (a,b,c) = (-65601, 83802, 67402),  a^3+b^3-c^3 = -1
  n = -2: (a,b,c) = (-791, 1010, 812),  a^3+b^3-c^3 = +1
  n = -1: (a,b,c) = (-9, 12, 10),  a^3+b^3-c^3 = -1
  [PASS] identity a_n^3+b_n^3-c_n^3 = (-1)^n holds at n = -3,-2,-1
  [PASS] (a_{-1}, b_{-1}, c_{-1}) = (-9, 12, 10)
  [PASS] (-9)^3 + 12^3 = 10^3 - 1  (n = -1, (-1)^n = -1)
  [PASS] rearranged: 9^3 + 10^3 = 12^3 + 1
  [PASS] 9^3 + 10^3 = 1729 = 12^3 + 1^3 (taxicab)
So (9,10,12) belongs to the SAME Ramanujan family, at index n = -1 of the
backward extension -- 1729 is not a companion parametrization but the
(-1)-st term of the lost-notebook family.  (Ono & Trebat-Leder 2015 make the
same observation en route to the K3 surface; see citation block below.)

==============================================================================
PART 4c -- PROOF FOR ALL n (Cayley-Hamilton on the Kronecker cube)
==============================================================================
  [PASS] det C = -1 (recurrence reversible over Z)
M = C(x)C(x)C built: 27x27 integer matrix.
  [PASS] chi_M has integer coefficients
chi_M(x) = x^27 + ... ; constant term chi_M(0) = 1
  [PASS] chi_M(0) != 0  (recurrence for d_n reversible)
  [PASS] chi_M(-1) = 0  (so (-1)^n satisfies the order-27 recurrence)
  [PASS] det M = -1 (M invertible over Z; (*) holds for all n in Z)
  [PASS] d_n = 0 for ALL n in [-10, 40]  (51 values, exact bigints)
  [PASS] order-27 recurrence (*) annihilates d_n on the window

PROVED (all n in Z, hence in particular all n >= 0):
    a_n^3 + b_n^3 = c_n^3 + (-1)^n
for Ramanujan's sequences, INCLUDING the backward extension; the n = -1
term is the taxicab identity 9^3 + 10^3 = 12^3 + 1 = 1729.
Proof inputs, all exact integer arithmetic: det C = -1; chi_M integral,
monic, chi_M(0) = 1, chi_M(-1) = 0; d_n = 0 at 51 >= 27
consecutive indices.  No floats anywhere.

==============================================================================
CITATIONS / READING
==============================================================================
- Ramanujan, lost notebook (the three generating functions; see also
  M. D. Hirschhorn, 'An amazing identity of Ramanujan', Math. Mag. 68 (1995)
  199-201, and Hirschhorn, Math. Mag. 69 (1996) 267-269 for proofs).
- K. Ono, S. Trebat-Leder, 'The 1729 K3 surface', Res. Number Theory (2015),
  2:26 (2016), arXiv:1510.00735: Ramanujan's near-miss family comes from his
  study of Euler's quartic surface X^3+Y^3 = Z^3+W^3; he had found (decades
  before the term existed) an elliptically-fibered K3 surface of Picard
  number 18 whose cubic twists give infinitely many elliptic curves over Q
  of rank >= 2; 1729 sits in the family (our n = -1).  [Full text not
  re-fetched this session -- web fetch failed; abstract + press coverage
  verified via search results, accessed 2026-06-10.]
- Eisenstein coda: x^3 + y^3 - z^3 = +-1 says the norm-form factorization
  (x+y)(x+wy)(x+w^2y) = z^3 +- 1 in Z[w], w = zeta_6 - 1... precisely:
  x^3+y^3 = N(x+wy)*(x+y) -- misses the Fermat cube z^3 by a UNIT of Z[w].
  Near-misses are unit-distance-from-Fermat statements; the unit group of
  Z[w] is finite (6 roots of unity), which is why +-1 is the only possible
  'miss' and why the family is infinite yet never closes the gap (FLT n=3,
  Euler/Gauss: x^3+y^3=z^3 has no nontrivial solution).

